# Play Atlas

A React 19 and TypeScript game discovery and personal-library app, built with
Vite and Tailwind CSS. IGDB catalogs power search, release listings, and details;
IsThereAnyDeal supplies US storefront prices. Personal records stay in the
browser's IndexedDB and localStorage. GitHub Actions builds data and deploys
the static app to GitHub Pages.

## Local setup

Use Node.js 22.13 or newer and npm:

```sh
npm ci
npm run dev
```

The checked-in catalog is a small sample. Production browsing needs the generated
browser catalog under public/data (including browser_catalog_manifest.json,
search/, releases/, and chunks/). To generate one, configure IGDB credentials in
.env.local, then run:

```sh
npm run build:igdb-full
npm run build:browser-catalog
```

These write generated/igdb-full-test and generated/browser-catalog-test. Set
PLAY_ATLAS_BROWSER_DATA_DIR=public/data when building a catalog for the local app,
or copy the generated browser catalog contents to public/data. Environment
variables can be set in .env.local, including on Windows. The build:igdb-test
script uses POSIX inline variable syntax; on PowerShell use
`$env:IGDB_IMPORT_MODE='test'; npx tsx scripts/build-igdb-index.ts` instead.
An optional VITE_CATALOG_PLUGIN_URL loads a development-only catalog module;
it is separate from pricing and is not included in production.

## Credentials and pricing

Create .env.local (ignored by Git) with the credentials you need:

```dotenv
IGDB_CLIENT_ID=your-twitch-client-id
IGDB_CLIENT_SECRET=your-twitch-client-secret
ITAD_API_KEY=your-isthereanydeal-api-key
```

ITAD_API_KEY must be the API key from your registered ITAD application, rather
than its OAuth client ID or client secret. The legacy ITAD_SECRET variable is
accepted for deployments that already store the API key under that name.
Never prefix these credentials with VITE_; that would expose them to browsers.

```sh
npm run build:deals
```

PLAY_ATLAS_DEALS_CATALOG_DIR explicitly selects the catalog directory. Without
it, ingestion tries generated/browser-catalog-rebuilt, generated/browser-catalog-test,
generated/igdb-full-test, then public/data. It supports importer envelopes,
legacy arrays, and raw/compressed full-catalog chunks. PLAY_ATLAS_DEALS_OUTPUT_DIR
selects where deals.json is written (default public/data).

Ingestion looks up native Steam, GOG, and Epic product IDs and stores ITAD's
current best US price per matched game. Failed requests, invalid responses,
missing credentials, or missing storefront mappings fail the command without
replacing the existing feed. A successful feed can include ordinary full prices
as well as discounts. Prices update when ingestion runs, not on every page visit.
API reference: https://docs.isthereanydeal.com/

## Checks and deployment

```sh
npm test
npm run lint
npm run build
```

The test suite includes mocked ITAD ingestion tests; live ingestion requires
credentials. Browser tests can be run with `npx playwright test` after installing
Playwright browsers. The production build goes to dist/.

The IGDB Database Pipeline Importer & Pages Deployment workflow runs nightly at
02:00 UTC and supports manual test, full, and deploy_existing operations. Configure
IGDB_CLIENT_ID, IGDB_CLIENT_SECRET, and ITAD_API_KEY (or legacy ITAD_SECRET) in
GitHub Actions secrets. Production runs use the github-pages environment, so
environment-scoped secrets must be available there. Test runs use repository
secrets. Missing pricing credentials now fail ingestion visibly.

Production workflows check pricing API access before starting the full import.
Transient network errors, rate limits, and server errors are retried. Unavailable
games omitted by lookup or price responses are skipped; entirely missing price
results still fail rather than replace the previous feed. The deployed feed comes
from the generated catalog, never from the checked-in sample.

If a downstream step fails, an Actions recovery catalog artifact is retained for
seven days. To retry without reimporting IGDB, start a new run on main with
operation deploy_existing and recovery_run_id set to the failed run's numeric ID.
This explicitly restores that run's artifact instead of an older release.

